description:
/crates/kith-store/src/lib.rs:103-146 (MIGRATIONS const and migrate function)

Current code:
  const MIGRATIONS: &[(u32, &str)] = &[(1, SCHEMA_V1)];

  fn migrate(conn: &Connection) -> Result<(), KithError> {
      let current: u32 = conn.query_row('PRAGMA user_version', [], |row| row.get(0))?;
      for &(version, sql) in MIGRATIONS {
          if current < version {
              conn.execute_batch(sql)?;
              conn.execute_batch(&format!('PRAGMA user_version = {version}'))?;
          }
      }
  }

Risk: MIGRATIONS array is assumed to be in ascending version order, but not enforced.
If someone adds:
  - Migration 1 (exists)
  - Migration 3 (NEW, added by one developer)
  - Migration 2 (added later by another developer)

The loop applies them in MIGRATIONS array order (1, 3, 2), not numerical order.
If Migration 3 depends on changes in Migration 2, this causes silent data corruption.

Solution: Either:
1. Sort MIGRATIONS at runtime: for &(v, sql) in MIGRATIONS.iter().sorted_by_key(|m| m.0)
2. Compile-time assertion that MIGRATIONS is sorted
3. Runtime panics with clear error message

Impact: Prevents accidental data corruption when adding new migrations.
